  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Suppose $a\\ne b$. Find the turning point of\n$$y=(a-b)x^2-2(a^2-b^2)x.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(a+b,\\,(b-a)(a+b)^2)$.\n\nUse $x=-\\dfrac{B}{2A}$, then substitute into the quadratic.",
      "worked": "The leading coefficient is $A=a-b\\ne0$, and $B=-2(a^2-b^2)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=-\\frac{B}{2A}\\\\x&=\\frac{2(a^2-b^2)}{2(a-b)}\\\\x&=a+b.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute $x=a+b$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=(a-b)(a+b)^2-2(a^2-b^2)(a+b)\\\\y&=(a-b)(a+b)^2-2(a-b)(a+b)^2\\\\y&=(b-a)(a+b)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe turning point is $(a+b,\\,(b-a)(a+b)^2)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 58
  },
